## Architecture: modular monolith
The API layer, transaction service, and alert service run as **one Spring
Boot application** — one deployable, one JVM. Treat them as separate
packages/modules with clean boundaries (see `backend-java.mdc`), not as
separate services. Do not introduce separate deployables, service mesh,
or inter-service HTTP calls between these three — that defeats the point
of the monolith.

The **rule engine** is the one piece designed to be pulled out and scaled
independently later (see `rule-engine.mdc`). Keep it decoupled behind a
clear interface even while it lives in the same process for MVP, so
extracting it later is a deployment change, not a rewrite.

## Build order — MVP first, always
Do not build phase-2 items before the MVP path works end to end:

1. REST API layer (transactions + rules read endpoints)
2. Transaction service (record + query)
3. Rule engine — **Amount Threshold rule only**, called synchronously
   (no queue yet)
4. Alert service — full lifecycle, not just creation
5. React UI — transaction list, alert list, alert detail, lifecycle actions

Phase 2 (do not start until the above works and is tested): Velocity /
New Payee / Daily Limit rules, async queue + horizontally scaled rule
engine workers, caching for count lookups, read replica, TLS/encryption
hardening beyond defaults.

## Development approach
Test-driven, agile. See `testing.mdc` — do not write implementation code
before a failing test exists for it, especially for rule engine logic.

## When planning a non-trivial feature
Before implementing anything more than a small fix, produce a short plan
and save it to `.cursor/plans/` (see that folder's README) before writing
code.
